<stack-static>
    <statement><p><p>The school games club buys footballs and netballs. Let <m>x</m> be the number of footballs and <m>y</m> the number of netballs. Write an inequality for each condition.</p><p>(a) Each of the footballs costs Ksh <m>1500</m> and each of the netballs costs Ksh <m>1100</m>; they can spend at most Ksh <m>21\,000</m>.</p><div><fillin characters="18" name="ans1"/>  </div><p>(b) There is room for at most <m>16</m> balls altogether.</p><div><fillin characters="18" name="ans2"/>  </div><p>(c) There must be at least <m>5</m> netballs.</p><div><fillin characters="18" name="ans3"/>  </div></p></statement>
    <solution><p><p>(a) The cost, or the amount used, is <m>{1500}x + {1100}y</m>, and ‘at most’ gives <m>\le</m>: <m>{1100 y+1500 x\leq 21000}</m>. Dividing by <m>{100}</m> gives the simpler form <m>{11 y+15 x\leq 210}</m>.</p><p>(b) The total number is <m>x + y</m>, and ‘at most’ gives <m>{y+x\leq 16}</m>.</p><p>(c) <m>{y\geq 5}</m>. The words decide the sign: at least <m>\ge</m>, more than <m>\gt</m>, fewer than <m>\lt</m>, at most <m>\le</m>. For ‘times as many’, check with numbers before you write the inequality.</p><p>Each limit on what is possible becomes one inequality. Any equivalent form, such as both sides multiplied by the same positive number, is accepted.</p></p></solution>
    <answer><p><m>1100 y+1500 x\leq 21000</m></p></answer>
    <answer><p><m>y+x\leq 16</m></p></answer>
    <answer><p><m>y\geq 5</m></p></answer>
</stack-static>